endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite44†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL157: \nL158: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite45†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL159: cite46†About†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L160: \nL161: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2512.14643] Improved Lower Bounds for QAC0 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.14643)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.14643\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Quantum Physics\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 16 Dec 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 25 Aug 2026 (this version, v4)]\nL9: # Title:Improved Lower Bounds for QAC0\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Malvika Raj Joshi , cite7†Avishay Tal , cite8†Francisca Vasconcelos , cite9†John Wright L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Improved Lower Bounds for QAC0, by Malvika Raj Joshi and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:In this work, we prove the strongest known lower bounds for QAC$^0$, allowing polynomially many gates and ancillae. Our main results show that:\nL17: > (1) Depth-3 QAC$^0$ circuits cannot compute PARITY, and require $\\Omega(\\exp(\\sqrt{n}))$ gates to compute MAJORITY.\nL18: > (2) Depth-2 circuits cannot approximate high-influence Boolean functions (e.g., PARITY) with non-negligible advantage, regardless of size.\nL19: > We develop new classical simulation techniques for QAC$^0$ to obtain our depth-3 bounds. In these results, we relax the output requirement of the quantum circuit to a single bit, making our depth $2$ approximation bound stronger than the previous best bound of Rosenthal (2021). This also enables us to draw natural comparisons with classical AC$^0$ circuits, which can compute PARITY exactly in depth $2$ (exp size).\nL20: Our techniques further suggest that, for boolean total functions, constant-depth quantum circuits do not necessarily provide more power than their classical counterparts. Our third result shows that depth $2$ QAC$^0$ circuits, regardless of size, cannot exactly synthesize an $n$-target nekomata state (a state whose synthesis is directly related to the computation of PARITY).\nL21: This complements the depth $2$ exponential size upper bound of Rosenthal (2021) for approximating nekomatas (which is used as a sub-circuit in the only known constant depth PARITY upper bound). Finally, we argue that approximating PARITY in QAC0, with significantly better than 1/poly(n) advantage on average, is just as hard as computing it exactly. Thus, extending our techniques to higher depths would also rule out approximate circuits for PARITY and related problems\nL22: Subjects:  | Quantum Physics (quant-ph); Computational Complexity (cs.CC)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2512.14643 [quant-ph]\nL24:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2512.14643v4 [quant-ph] for this version)\nL25:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.14643†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: Journal reference:  | STOC 2026: Proceedings of the 58th Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing, 2199-2209\nL27: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite15†https://doi.org/10.1145/3798129.3800922†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL28: ## Submission history\nL29: \nL30: From: Malvika Raj Joshi [cite